    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • 2027 Hyundai Bayon Finally Looks More Like a Box and Less Like an Egg\n1965 Ford Mustang Fastback Is a Shelby GT 350R Tribute With 'Extra Seasoning.' It Won't Sell Cheap\n10 photos\nPhoto: Garage Kept Motors / Instagram\nSitting proudly in the inventory of Grand Rapids, Michigan-based Garage Kept Motors , this spectacular 1965 Mustang Fastback Shelby GT 350R tribute looks readily dressed to impress. But it won’t come home with you unless you’re bankable…\nFord's longest nameplate in continuous production, the Mustang , sold ‘just’ 9,960 units across the US automotive market during the third quarter of the year, with 38,685 examples of the breed sold yar-to-date, up almost 18% compared to last year. That’s a far cry for the S650 iteration, the seventh by its name, compared to the 1965 Mustang, which sold over 400k units in its first year of availability!\nBut hey, times were different back then, and it’s really no surprise that a lot of people continue to praise the ’65 Mustang over anything else. But how about a modified one? Well, here’s “a classic example of the first-generation Mustang transformed into a purpose-built performance machine,” with this 1965 Ford Mustang Fastback rocking a frame-off custom build, according to the dealer, as well as “a striking blue and orange livery inspired by the competition Mustangs of the era.”\nThe classic livery even features “some new old stock decals,” and the car also comes with the period-correct \"extractor\" rear window with integrated center brake light or an in-trunk fuel cell with \"gas hole.\" All in all, the description says it’s a “pure old school race car with just enough street car sprinkled on and the power and control to take the GT350R experience to Spinal Tap levels at a quality level worthy of the cover of the January 2025 issue of Hemmings Muscle Machines.”\nThat’s specific, right? Anyway, here are the details. The fastback comes with a “302-based oversquare 363ci Small Block Ford V8 featuring quad Weber 48s and forged internals, pumping out north of 500 horsepower,” a five-speed Tremec TKX with overdrive for “comfortable street driving,” a custom chassis, a street or track coilover suspension, and disc brakes with a three-link rear conversion fitted with a 3.89 9-inch rear end featuring a Detroit Locker plus a front rack and pinion conversion.\nThe alloys are 17-inch Minilite-style rims, and now there’s also a side exhaust breathing through a set of long tubes and Spintech mufflers “for the right sound,” while the Gulf Oil livery is contrasted by the black interior featuring a “four-point roll bar, Cypher adjustable buckets, four-point harnesses, and carbon Autometer Ultra-Lite gauges.”\nNaturally, this crazy build (created by MotorCity Grind of Detroit, Michigan) isn’t selling cheap. After all, it was featured in the #257 January 2025 Issue Hemmings Muscle Machines magazine as their cover car. Well, the damage to the bank account is quite obvious: the dealership wants no less than $184,900 to part ways with this particular example. Cool or not?\nView this post on Instagram A post shared by Garage Kept Motors LLC (@garagekeptmotors)\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\n1965 Ford Mustang Fastback Ford Mustang Ford Mustang Shelby GT 350R tribute car for sale Garage Kept Motors\nAbout the author: Aurel Niculescu\nAurel has aimed high all his life (literally, at 16 he was flying gliders all by himself) so in 2006 he switched careers and got hired as a writer at his favorite magazine. Since then, his work has been published both by print and online outlets, most recently right here, on autoevolution. Full profile\nUP NEXT 8-Second 1/4-Mile King for Sale: This Dodge Challenger SRT Demon 170 Is Brand New",
